quantum.problem:
A helium atom contains two electrons, each with a charge of \(-e\), and a nuclear charge of \(2e\). Calculate the ground state energy of the helium atom, assuming the most probable radius is \(r \sim R\), and using the following averages:
\[
\left\langle \frac{1}{r_1} \right\rangle = \left\langle \frac{1}{r_2} \right\rangle \approx \frac{1}{R}, \quad \left\langle \frac{1}{r_{12}} \right\rangle \approx \frac{1}{2R}
\]
According to the uncertainty principle, we have:
\[
\left\langle \boldsymbol{p}_1^2 \right\rangle = \left\langle \boldsymbol{p}_2^2 \right\rangle \approx \frac{\hbar^2}{R^2}
\]
and utilizing the following total energy operator:
\begin{equation*}
H = \frac{1}{2m} \left(p_1^2 + p_2^2\right) - 2e^2 \left(\frac{1}{r_1} + \frac{1}{r_2}\right) + \frac{e^2}{r_{12}}
\end{equation*}

An atom has four lowest energy levels:

\[ E_{0}=-14.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{1}=-9.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{2}=-7.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{3}=-5.5 \, \mathrm{eV} \]

The transition rate \(A_{ij}\) (Einstein coefficient) for the transition \(i \rightarrow j\) is

\[
\begin{aligned}
& A_{20} = 1.2 \times 10^{8} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}, \quad A_{21} = 8.0 \times 10^{7} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
\]

Calculate the radiative lifetime of the \(E_{2}\) energy level.

An atom has four lowest energy levels:

\[ E_{0}=-14.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{1}=-9.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{2}=-7.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{3}=-5.5 \, \mathrm{eV} \]

The transition rate \(A_{ij}\) (Einstein coefficient) for the transition \(i \rightarrow j\) is given by:

\[
\begin{aligned}
& A_{10} = 3.0 \times 10^{8} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}, \quad A_{20} = 1.2 \times 10^{8} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1} \\
& A_{21} = 8.0 \times 10^{7} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
\]

Consider a container containing a large number of atoms in the energy level \(E_{2}\). Find the ratio of the energy emitted per unit time for the transition \(E_{2} \rightarrow E_{0}\) to the transition \(E_{2} \rightarrow E_{1}\).

